# Error Handling Rules

## Mutation Error Propagation

### NEVER swallow errors inside mutationFn
```typescript
// ❌ Forbidden — swallows error, onSuccess fires with undefined
mutationFn: async (data) => {
  try {
    return await entityApi.create(data);
  } catch (e) {
    console.error(e); // error eaten here
  }
}

// ✅ Required — let errors propagate, handle in onError
mutationFn: (data) => entityApi.create(data),
onError: (error) => { /* show toast or set error state */ },
```

## Required onError Handler for Mutations
Every `useMutation` that modifies server state MUST have an `onError` handler:
```typescript
useMutation({
  mutationFn: entityApi.create,
  onSuccess: () => {
    queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ["entity"] });
  },
  onError: (error) => {
    // show user feedback: toast, inline message, etc.
  },
});
```

## Forbidden: console.error in Production Code
```typescript
// ❌ Forbidden in models/ and logics/ files
catch (e) {
  console.error(e);
}

// ✅ Use error state from TanStack Query — it already captures the error
const { error } = useQuery({ ... });
// Pass error to UI via return value
return { error, isLoading, data };
```

## Required: All 4 UI States Returned from Models
Every model hook that uses `useQuery` must expose all 4 states to the UI:
```typescript
// ✅ Required pattern
export const useEntityModel = () => {
  const { data, isLoading, error, refetch } = useQuery({ ... });
  return {
    data,
    isLoading,
    error,       // must be exposed
    onRetry: refetch, // UI needs a retry trigger
  };
};

// ❌ Forbidden — hiding error state
export const useEntityModel = () => {
  const { data, isLoading } = useQuery({ ... }); // error omitted
  return { data, isLoading };
};
```

## ApiError Handling
API methods from `@/config/api` throw `ApiError` on non-2xx responses.
NEVER catch and re-throw as a generic Error:
```typescript
// ❌ Loses structured error info
try {
  return await entityApi.getAll();
} catch (e) {
  throw new Error("Failed"); // ApiError fields lost
}

// ✅ Let TanStack Query catch it — error state will be ApiError instance
queryFn: () => entityApi.getAll(),
```
